<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="8dc45a72">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of the change management process is to constructively manage change. The change management process
    identifies how scope/work product changes will be identified, classified, communicated and integrated into the
    project.&lt;br /&gt;
    Change management is an expected and integral part of the iterative discovery and implementation process.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    A good change control approach allows for continuous, real-time adjustments to provide for the relevancy and accuracy
    of the final deliverables.&lt;br /&gt;
    Innovative change should provide positive results that outweigh the original cost of making the change.&lt;br /&gt;
    Project scope is managed by the project charter and change requests.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Project charter&lt;/strong&gt; - Specifies the baseline scope agreement between the project team and the project
        sponsor.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Change requests&lt;/strong&gt; - Outline any subsequent changes to this baseline scope throughout the duration of
        the project. Consequently, the current project scope is determined by the sum of the baseline scope defined in the
        project charter and any approved change requests.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The objectives of project change management are to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Confirm&amp;nbsp;coordination of activities across stakeholders/organizations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Provide a standardized, controlled, reliable process for identifying and tracking changes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Help project teams anticipate and minimize the occurrence of any situation that reduces project value:
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Confirm implementation of necessary and acceptable changes only
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Formalize the process of management review and approval of change requests
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Maintain formal change request documentation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Confirm approval to perform detailed analysis before substantial time is invested
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Create a communication vehicle to address business impacts
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Protect the program and project’s scope from uncontrolled change and distraction
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Enable controlled and efficient tracking of all changes over the life of the project
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Minimize the need for rework during or at the end of a project
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The diagram below depicts the states that a change goes through before the change can be considered ‘Closed’.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/Change.png&quot; width=&quot;676&quot; height=&quot;372&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A change request is the way to inform the management team and get a resolution when:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Overall project schedule is going to slip and an extension to the schedule is needed.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Overall project costs are going to exceed the project’s budget.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more project deliverables need to be added/ removed from the project.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more milestones need to have its target completion date adjusted.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        One or more additional resources are required in order to complete the project (generally, this is related to
        increases in project costs).&lt;br /&gt;
        &lt;br /&gt;
        Changes to be implemented during the project lifecycle will require the approval of the Change Control Review Board
        (CCRB) and the sponsor.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Change management activities, resource requirements and work products should be integrated in the overall project scope
    and approach.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
